dies  since these terms really come from  environments so the envo ontology is  from the oboe foundry and it's a higher  hierarchical classification system for  classifying samples by environment  so one of these environmental  descriptors is the broad scale  environmental context and this term  should  come from the envo ontology which is  referenced on the slide as i have  demonstrated here you can see  um that you can select the biome  you would select a biome that the sample  originated from  um  and in this example  we're selecting the urban biome to  indicate that the sample originated from  people who spend the bulk of their time  in an urbanized area and if that's not  exactly what we need or we don't have  evidence that  the individuals in our study  spend most of their time in the  urbanized areas we'd want to use a more  generalized broader term that's above it  in this nested tree structure  now in addition to those broad terms  there's a local scale environmental  context which for these terms for human  associated microbiomes we'd actually  want to use uh uber  anatomy ontology or uber on which again  is linked at the bottom of this page and  here i have an example  of the tree structure  from an anatomical entity  and you can see that we can use the  terms lower urinary tract and if we have  a  specific catheter collected specimen we  could go more specific and say that that  sample came from the urinary bladder  and additionally there's environmental  medium terms which should also come from  the uber on ontology for human  associated microbiome studies and here i  have an example of the logical tree  structure for urine which indicates that  the data originated from a urine  specimen  so at this point i have introduced  several of the microbiome metadata  community standards and i've given some  examples of how they're used in  neurobiome research  now i'll review  the current status of these standards  and how we need to adapt them  which is a evolving and ongoing process  so the eurobiome research field is still  relatively young compared to other  microbiomes that have been studied for  decades and to effectively study the  microbiome we need expertise from  clinical specialists such as urologists  urogynecologists nephrologists and  pediatric urologists we also need to  make sure that we're capturing several  experts from biology such as  microbiologists and bacteriologists  and  virologists  and also the analytical sciences such as  chemistry biochemistry bioinformatics  and  statistics  so we need to make sure that we're all  working together and communicating  effectively  with each other to achieve the goals as  a community which is primarily to  improve the health care for patients  with low urinary tract disorders and  towards this goal the eurobiome research  community has begun to form standards  and consensus  to make sure that we're all educating  each other and this consensus statement  was published  in m systems last year  so now first and foremost  we must always keep in mind that  at some point the data that we have came  from a human subject so it may or may  not fall under the realm of human  subjects research depending on what data  we're using and what information was  collected so we need to make sure that  when we're sharing data we're paying  particular attention to  the rules of the institutional  review boards that oversee human  subjects research and that we make sure  we're very very very careful that we  only share de-identified data  and this means that there's definitely a  balance between being comprehensive and  also protecting privacy i know when i  look at some of the mandatory mix s  fields a lot of times i actually can't  share the information  that's required on those fields because  of the human protection  because the human protections that are  in place for the human subjects research  and  um the the big thing that i commonly  encounter is dates typically dates  cannot be shared uh just because they  might contain sensitive information that  may not be obvious to you or the  researcher  um  and often there are broad policies that  no dates can be shared um just kind of  as a blanket policy and this means that  any dates that we export need to be  either  uh have some sort of offset or be  generic such as just using a month and a  year only the same goes for location  location um particularly if it's tied to  the participant's home address cannot be  shared even in metadata and another one  that i commonly um see is age over 90  might not be obvious to everyone that  that could be sensitive information and  is often not terrible and there are  several others this is by no means  comprehensive  another particular point for urobiome  research is that the terminology we use  when investigating the microbiome  we need really need to start being very  uh consistent because it's really key  for communicating and interpreting  results and the literature is already  getting uh quite a bit murky with if  we're studying the urinary urine or  bladder microbiome and we need to make  sure that the community is agreeing on  definitions for these types of micro  microbiomes that we're studying which is  often tied to the sample type that we  collected  so now another issue that i've  encountered  in my research is that the terminology i  need to describe the diseases or  disorders that i'm studying  is not really accurately represented in  the recommended disease and phenotype  ontologies that are out there um here's  one example so i study urgency urinary  incontinence  or urge urinary incontinence and when i  try to find that in the existing  ontologies  i'm giving the i'm given the options of  either urinary incontinence or stress  urinary incontinence and stress urinary  incontinence is a very distinct um  different type of disorder and urinary  incontinence is really too broad so i'm  not able to accurately  uh capture urge urinary incontinence  which is then unfortunate for  future research because people  are unable to search and find samples  that are associated with that term  and similarly when i look up urinary  urgency  i receive this definition that  incorporates  incontinence but that's not really quite  correct because you can have urinary  urgency in the absence of incontinence  so again these are  you know issues with the existing  ontologies that we need to work as a  community to  fill these gaps and make sure that we're  accurately describing um and how the  terms available that we need to us  so many of the concepts and guidelines  that i just discussed have been  published in this durability consensus  paper by brubeck baker at all um that  was published last year um this work  came out of the eurobiome 2020  conference that was held prior to the  pandemic where several researchers in  the urobiome field from different  backgrounds met and discussed these  details to try to develop a consensus  for this field  and  importantly we have several tables about  terminology and then also  metadata that should be collected or  elements that should be collected as  part of your biome research as i'm  displaying here which is from the table  one and as you can see here we have a  lot of biological elements  such as age sex and antibiotic use and  we also indicated if we think they are  required or desired for eurobiome  research  and as you can see this maps to the  variables  that we kind of discussed in the mixed x  packages  and here you can see that there is a  in-depth description or definition and  then also recommendations for terms for  investigators to use  and as part of this manuscript we also  included  a template in the supplemental material  that can be downloaded and used by  researchers and my group has also been  working on a redcap version that's near  completion where researchers can use  this to capture  the data in a standardized form  and it's compatible with the mix x data  standards  so in conclusion i hope you learned  about metadata standards and  applications for eurobiome research if  you'd like to gain a practical hands-on  experience digging into these details of  metadata for your about research please  join the eurobiome metadata workshop on  may 11th  registration will have a chat so please  only register  if you plan on attending and register  early to make sure that you can secure  your spot  also the euro volume standards are still  in development so please join the  conversation if you're in this area of  research or if you have expertise that  you think you can contribute to this  area of research we do have a eurobiome  101 workshop  that's coming up  at the  oggs ayuga  meeting in june and we are looking  forward to having another eurobiome  conference in 2023  there's also a caribou connections  working group for eurobond research so  please get in touch with me and i can  put you in touch with um  those folks as well  so with that i'd like to thank all of  you  you
 

